In the modern Spring IoC container, dependency injection is the cornerstone of inversion of control, allowing beans to be wired together dynamically. However, as applications scale and multiple candidate beans of the same type emerge within the application context, ambiguity arises. The Spring framework and Jakarta EE standards provide distinct annotations to resolve these injection points deterministically. Understanding the interplay between @Autowired, @Qualifier, @Primary, @Resource, and @Inject is critical for architecting robust enterprise systems without falling victim to unresolvable NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException failures.

@Autowired is Spring's native annotation for performing by-type autowiring on constructors, fields, and setter methods. When multiple beans match the requested type, Spring evaluates additional modifiers to narrow down the candidate pool. By contrast, @Qualifier acts as a targeted selective filter, allowing developers to specify an explicit bean name or custom qualifier value at the injection site to resolve ambiguities. Meanwhile, @Primary offers a declarative way to designate a default bean candidate globally, telling the container which instance to inject when no specific qualifier is provided. Beyond Spring-specific APIs, Jakarta EE specifications provide standardized alternatives: @Inject, part of JSR-330, serves as a standard alternative to @Autowired with subtle differences in lifecycle and behavior, while @Resource (from JSR-250) fundamentally resolves dependencies primarily by-name first, falling back to by-type resolution.

Mastering these annotations requires understanding their resolution precedence. When an injection point is encountered, Spring evaluates qualifiers first, looks for primary candidates second, and inspects bean name alignments third depending on the annotation semantics. Misusing these mechanisms often leads to runtime configuration crashes or silent dependency miswirings that are notoriously difficult to debug in large-scale enterprise microservices.

public interface PaymentService {
    void processPayment();
}

@Component
@Primary
public class CreditCardPaymentService implements PaymentService {
    @Override
    public void processPayment() { /* ... */ }
}

@Component
@Qualifier("paypal")
public class PayPalPaymentService implements PaymentService {
    @Override
    public void processPayment() { /* ... */ }
}

@Component
public class OrderManager {
    // Injects PayPalPaymentService explicitly via @Qualifier
    @Autowired
    @Qualifier("paypal")
    private PaymentService paypalService;

    // Injects CreditCardPaymentService because it is marked @Primary
    @Inject
    private PaymentService defaultService;

    // Injects by-name using Jakarta EE standard @Resource
    @Resource(name = "creditCardPaymentService")
    private PaymentService cardService;
}
